---
import { journey } from "../../data/journey";
import { stateOptions } from "../../lib/states";
import { outcomes } from "../../lib/parts";
import PartHead from "./PartHead.astro";
import AiStudy from "./AiStudy.astro";
import { carryPrompt } from "../../lib/primer";

const current = journey.flatMap((stage) => stage.modules).find((m) => m.slug === "01-collection")!;
const nextModule = journey.flatMap((stage) => stage.modules).find((m) => m.slug === "02-archive")!;
const ask = current.ask;
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
const film = (name: string) => `${base}film/${name}`;
const heads = [
  ["total", "100 visits across the network"],
  ["floor", "At least 100 visits. Verified reports from eight of ten clinics."],
  ["zeros", "100 visits. Two clinics reported none."],
  ["unknown", "Visits unknown"],
];
const next = [
  ["../01-collection-field-work/?from=carry-it#assistant", "Test your own AI assistant", "Give an assistant Friday's pages, see whether it catches the hidden gap, and leave with a reusable brief"],
  ["../01-collection-field-work/?from=carry-it#statistics", "Statistics of the gap", "Survey terms for each hole, where an expected set comes from, and linked bounds"],
  ["../01-collection-field-work/?from=carry-it#platform", "How Touchline builds it", "Incremental merges, atomic writes, and conditional requests"],
  ["../01-collection-field-work/?from=carry-it#lab", "The Python lab", "Write Friday's coverage record in code (~15 min)"],
];
---

<section id="carry-it">
  <PartHead id="carry-it">Carry it: same number, three worlds</PartHead>
  <p>The same rules work outside sports: count against an expected set, tell empty from unavailable, and bound what is missing.</p>
  <p>A health network requests a week 9 visit report from each of its ten clinics. For week 9, eight clinics send verified counts totaling 100 visits. Those eight rows are the same in three worlds. Only what you know about the other two clinics changes.</p>
  <div class="cv-twins">
    <div>
      <h4>World A</h4>
      <p>The other two clinics each sent a file labeled week 9, with a header and no rows. Both were closed that week for renovations, and the closure notices are on file.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h4>World B</h4>
      <p>The other two clinics sent nothing. Nobody has heard from them.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h4>World C</h4>
      <p>The other two each sent a file with a header and no rows, but nothing in either file says which week it covers, and there is no closure notice.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="cv-check" data-once="transfer">
    <p class="cv-kind">Transfer</p>
    {(["a", "b"] as const).map((w) => (
      <div class="cv-line" data-q data-type="select" data-answer={w === "a" ? "total|zeros" : "floor"}
        data-why={w === "a" ? "100 visits across the network, whether or not the headline mentions the two closed clinics. Their files are labeled week 9 and the closure notices confirm they saw nobody: Empty, so zero is true." : "At least 100 visits, from eight of ten. The two clinics that never answered are Unavailable: unknown, not zero."}
        data-wrong={JSON.stringify(w === "a" ? { floor: "World A's two clinics did answer, with proof. Nothing is unknown.", unknown: "Every clinic is accounted for, so the total is known." } : { total: "Two clinics never answered, so 100 is only what you received.", zeros: "Nobody reported zero. Silence is unknown, not zero.", unknown: "You do know it is at least 100. Say that." })}>
        <label class="cv-stack"><span>World {w.toUpperCase()}: which headline is honest?</span>
          <select data-save={`transfer-${w}`}><option value="">Choose</option>{heads.map(([k, t]) => <option value={k}>{t}</option>)}</select>
        </label>
        <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
      </div>
    ))}
    <div class="cv-line" data-q data-type="select" data-answer="unavailable" data-why="Unavailable. Files were saved, but nothing shows they cover week 9: the same rule as Case 3's statistics page that can't show its season." data-wrong={JSON.stringify({ empty: "Empty requires verifying which week the file covers. These files don't.", "no-collector": "The collector read the files' headers, so it can read the format. The files just can't show their week.", complete: "Complete needs rows and a verified week. These files have neither.", none: "The network expects a report from all ten clinics, so somebody asked." })}>
      <label class="cv-stack"><span>World C: what coverage state are those two clinics in for week 9?</span>
        <select data-save="transfer-c"><option value="">Choose a state</option>{stateOptions.map(([k, n]) => <option value={k}>{n}</option>)}</select>
      </label>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <div class="cv-q-sep" data-q data-type="number" data-answer="110,230" data-why="110 to 230. The low end is what you have. The high end adds the most the silent clinic can see within its license: 5 days × 24 visits = 120. Like Case 2's bound on Dunmore's points, it comes from a rule and says nothing about which total is likely.">
      <p class="cv-qlabel">For week 10, nine clinics send verified counts totaling 110 visits. The tenth hasn't answered. It operates within its license, which allows at most 24 visits a day on at most five days a week. What bound belongs on the network's week 10 total?</p>
      <div class="cv-row cv-fillin"><span>Between</span><input inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" aria-label="Lowest possible total" data-save="clinic-low" /><span>and</span><input inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" aria-label="Highest possible total" data-save="clinic-high" /></div>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-once">Answer all four, then check.</p>
    <button type="button" data-submit>Check answers</button>
    <p class="cv-fb" role="status" data-need></p>
    <div class="cv-reveal">
      <p>World A's two clinics are Empty, so zero is true. World B's and World C's are Unavailable. World C's files are header-only, like World A's, yet support only World B's headline, because nothing in them shows their week. A blank report justifies zero only when it is verified as the period you asked for, as World A's are. Same 100 visits, three worlds, and only the coverage record tells them apart.</p>
      <AiStudy
        id="carry-ai-study"
        title="Same rows, different conclusions"
        note="Send this prompt to read the reply an AI assistant gave, or copy it into your own. It moves the three worlds to warehouses, shows the record entry for each, and ends with three new questions."
        prompt={carryPrompt}
        href="../01-collection-ai-carry/"
      />
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 id="carry-it-recap">What you can now do</h3>
  <ul class="cv-outcomes">
    {outcomes.map((o) => <li>{o.can} <a href={o.href}>{o.where}</a></li>)}
  </ul>
  <p class="cv-done-note"><span data-progress-text></span> <a href="#setup" data-rail-resume="name" hidden></a></p>

  <h3 id="carry-it-prompt">The question to take with you</h3>
  <p>Ask this whenever an AI collects or summarizes data for you:</p>
  <div class="cv-user-prompt cv-takeaway-prompt" role="region" aria-label="Reusable coverage prompt">
    <div class="cv-chat-bar">
      <span class="cv-chat-badge">Prompt</span>
      <span class="cv-chat-meta">You → any AI</span>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-chat-text" data-copy-text>{ask}</p>
    <button type="button" data-copy data-label="Copy prompt">Copy prompt</button>
  </div>

  <h3 id="carry-it-tuesday">Then Tuesday</h3>
  <p>This short closing video returns to what actually happened: Monday's corrected table, and the bug that kicks off Module 2.</p>
  <figure class="cv-film">
    <video controls playsinline preload="metadata" poster={film("monday-then-tuesday.jpg")} aria-label="Monday, Then Tuesday, a short closing video for Module 1">
      <source src={film("monday-then-tuesday.mp4")} type="video/mp4" />
      <track kind="captions" src={film("monday-then-tuesday.vtt")} srclang="en" label="English" default />
    </video>
    <figcaption>Monday, Then Tuesday. Every count shown on screen comes from the sample dataset.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <aside class="cv-next-card" aria-label="Next module">
    <p class="cv-next-eyebrow">Up next · Module 2</p>
    <p class="cv-next-q">{nextModule.question}</p>
    <p>Module 2 begins with Riley's Tuesday question: “Can you rebuild Friday's table from the pages you kept, without asking a single site again, and show me you did?”</p>
    <a class="cv-next-cta" href="../02-archive/">Continue to Module 2: {nextModule.name} →</a>
  </aside>

  <h3 id="carry-it-further">Optional: go further</h3>
  <p>Each of these stands on its own on the <a href="../01-collection-field-work/?from=carry-it">Module 1 field work page</a>.</p>
  <ul class="cv-next-links">
    {next.map(([href, name, what]) => <li><a href={href}>{name}</a><span>{what}</span></li>)}
  </ul>
</section>
